---
// The one feature that is not in the app yet: FRC RAG. The section shows a real FRC RAG answer,
// rebuilt in HTML from the screenshot in the launch post, so it is sharp and in the page's colours.
// The words are the tool's own. The picture is the intake from team 694's 2020 technical binder,
// which is the source the answer cites.
// The answer sits on a dark panel with a blue glow, like the panels in FeatureRows.
---

<section class="band" id="soon">
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="head">
      <h2><span>Coming soon.</span> Feedback grounded in what other teams built.</h2>
      <p>
        The reviewer will draw on FRC RAG, which answers from 64 technical binders written over 10+ years of FRC. When
        it flags a problem, it will show how other teams already solved it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <div class="art">
        <div class="chat">
          <p class="question">How have teams made an efficient transfer to bring balls from an intake to a shooter?</p>
          <p class="reasoned"><i></i>Reasoned for a few seconds</p>
          <div class="answer">
            <h3>How teams typically transfer power cells from an intake to a shooter</h3>
            <blockquote>
              &ldquo;The 694-2020 design shows the intake itself &ndash; a roller-based system that feeds balls into an
              indexer. The transfer from that indexer to the shooter is a natural extension of the same
              principles.&rdquo;
            </blockquote>
            <img
              class="source"
              src="/landing/frc-rag-intake.webp"
              alt="A CAD render of team 694's 2020 intake: rollers between two plates, on a four-bar linkage"
              width="520"
              height="326"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <ul>
              <li>rollers + polycarbonate plates</li>
              <li>four-bar linkage that deploys the <span class="keep">intake <span class="cite">1</span></span></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Below is a practical, step-by-step guide that many teams use (or can adapt) to make this
              transfer <b>efficient, reliable, and easy to tune</b>.
            </p>
            <p class="next">1. Design Overview</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>FRC RAG on its own today, answering from team 694&rsquo;s 2020 binder.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  figure {
    margin: 56px 0 0;
  }

  .art {
    position: relative;
    padding: 56px 24px 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--divide);
    border-radius: var(--round-panel);
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #151617;
  }

  /* A blue glow rising from the bottom edge, behind the answer. */
  .art::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: radial-gradient(60% 46% at 50% 108%, rgb(72 132 230 / 0.3), transparent);
  }

  /* The answer runs on past what is shown, so the window meets the bottom of the card, and the
     heading of the answer's next part fades out under a rule. */
  .chat {
    position: relative;
    max-width: 620px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 24px 26px 0;
    border: 1px solid #333334;
    border-bottom: 0;
    border-radius: var(--round-card) var(--round-card) 0 0;
    background: #17181a;
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px -12px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .question {
    width: fit-content;
    max-width: 78%;
    margin: 0 0 22px auto;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-radius: var(--round-card);
    background: var(--tint);
  }

  .reasoned {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    padding: 9px 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--divide);
    border-radius: var(--round-tile);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .reasoned i {
    width: 7px;
    height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  /* A chevron at the far end, as on the collapsed reasoning row in FRC RAG. */
  .reasoned::after {
    content: "";
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    margin: 0 4px 3px auto;
    border-right: 1.5px solid var(--text-2);
    border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--text-2);
    rotate: 45deg;
  }

  .answer {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(#000 84%, transparent);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(#000 84%, transparent);
  }

  h3 {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    padding-left: 14px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--line-hover);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  .source {
    display: block;
    width: 220px;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto 18px;
    border-radius: var(--round-tile);
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    padding-left: 18px;
  }

  li::marker {
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  /* Keeps the citation mark on the same line as the word before it. */
  .keep {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The citation mark that points at the binder. */
  .cite {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    padding: 0 5px;
    border-radius: var(--round-chip);
    background: var(--tint);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--accent);
    vertical-align: 1px;
  }

  .answer p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .answer .next {
    margin-top: 22px;
    padding: 20px 0 14px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--divide);
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  b {
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 14px;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    figure {
      margin-top: 36px;
    }

    .art {
      padding: 28px 12px 0;
    }

    .chat {
      padding: 18px 16px 0;
    }

    .question {
      max-width: 92%;
    }
  }
</style>
